  "summary": "Aurora (CO) police installed Flock ALPR cameras at nine high schools in 2025; five remain at Aurora Public Schools campuses under a formal agreement, while four at Cherry Creek Schools were removed in May after the district found no authorizing agreement existed. The article details the department's retention policy, privacy concerns raised by advocates about surveilling minors, and situates the dispute within a broader Colorado and national backlash against Flock.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Aurora Public Schools entered into a formal agreement with the police department allowing the cameras on its campuses, and that agreement requires them to stay in place for at least two years while being installed at no cost to the district.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "Cherry Creek's Board of Education directed the removal of the cameras from those four campuses this past May, after the district determined there was no official agreement with the Aurora Police Department covering them.",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "Aurora Police Department's automated-license-plate-reader directive says plate-scan information is retained for one year",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "Jojo Padilla said the cameras eliminate the ability of children and their parents to consent to being monitored.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    }
  ],
